.TH CHTTPSVR_ROUTER_USE 3 "2026-08-03" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpsvr_router_use \- append middleware to a sub-router
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpserver.h>

ccol_retval_t chttpsvr_router_use(chttpsvr_router *router,
                                   chttpsvr_middleware_fn fn, void *ctx);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Appends a middleware to the chain of the given sub-router.  The
middleware runs after the global middleware and before the route
handler, and only for requests that the server routes through this
sub-router.

It also runs for a request that is routed to this sub-router and then
rejected with 413 before its handler could run, and for the 404 or 405
of a path that this sub-router owns but none of its routes matches.
See chttpsvr_subrouter(3) for which paths a sub-router owns, and
chttpsvr_use(3) for the full rule and for the rejections that run no
middleware at all.

This function is thread-safe and can be called at any time, even while
another thread runs chttpsvr_destroy on the owning server; a call that
races such a destroy returns ccol_invalid_args and touches no freed
memory.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The NOTES section of chttpsvr_use describes three further points: the
cap that each router applies at registration time, the combined limit
of 32 middleware steps per request at dispatch time, and the policy
that the first of two duplicate routes wins.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success on success.
ccol_invalid_args when router or fn is NULL, or when the server that
                  owns router has been destroyed.
ccol_not_enough_memory when an allocation fails.
ccol_not_permitted when the middleware chain of this router is already
at the cap of 32 entries.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpsvr_use (3),
.BR chttpsvr_subrouter (3)
